Emscripten 4.0.22, system/lib/pthread/proxying.c: signal a synchronous
proxied call's condition variable before unlocking its mutex, as upstream
does since 5.0.5 (emscripten-core/emscripten#26582, "Fixed a race condition
in syscall proxying that caused some hangs and ASan errors"). Applied by
build.sh to the emsdk clangd is linked with.

Every file system and stdout call clangd's threads make is proxied
synchronously to the Worker that loaded clangd (server.js), with the
em_proxying_ctx on the calling thread's stack. emscripten_proxy_finish set
DONE and unlocked, then signalled. In between, the caller could take the
mutex, see DONE without ever waiting, return, and reuse that stack; the
signal then ran musl's pthread_cond_signal on whatever was there and
followed its waiter list. An odd "pointer" there made Wasm trap on server.js
with "operation does not support unaligned accesses" (an uncaught error in
the LSP Worker, so a page error, about one C++ e2e run in four while the
machine was busy); an aligned one corrupted memory or spun in the cond's lock.
cancel_ctx had the same order. QEMU (vm/build-qemu.sh) uses 6.0.13, which
has the fix. test-proxying.mjs (tests/proxying-race/race.c) catches the old order.

--- a/system/lib/pthread/proxying.c
+++ b/system/lib/pthread/proxying.c
@@ -339,8 +339,11 @@
     pthread_mutex_lock(&ctx->sync.mutex);
     ctx->sync.state = DONE;
     remove_active_ctx(ctx);
-    pthread_mutex_unlock(&ctx->sync.mutex);
+    // Signal must come before unlock to avoid emscripten_proxy_sync_with ctx
+    // seeing the state as DONE and freeing the ctx before we call unlock.
+    // See https://github.com/emscripten-core/emscripten/pull/26582
     pthread_cond_signal(&ctx->sync.cond);
+    pthread_mutex_unlock(&ctx->sync.mutex);
   } else {
     // Schedule the callback on the caller thread. If the caller thread has
     // already died or dies before the callback is executed, then at least make
@@ -365,8 +368,9 @@
   if (ctx->kind == SYNC) {
     pthread_mutex_lock(&ctx->sync.mutex);
     ctx->sync.state = CANCELED;
-    pthread_mutex_unlock(&ctx->sync.mutex);
+    // Signal must be first, see comment in emscripten_proxy_finish.
     pthread_cond_signal(&ctx->sync.cond);
+    pthread_mutex_unlock(&ctx->sync.mutex);
   } else {
     if (ctx->cb.cancel == NULL ||
         !do_proxy(ctx->cb.queue,
